etric reductant and byproducts, so it should not be represented as a simple bimolecular conversion. rxn_004 is again an incomplete hydrolysis step.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Encode rxn_005 as thiophosphate-driven UV photoredox chemistry, not as a simple bimolecular condensation.\",\n        \"Add full stoichiometry for redox equivalents and byproducts in rxn_005.\",\n        \"Correct rxn_004 hydrolysis bookkeeping and environment label.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.08,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.3\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.12,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.3,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.43,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.16,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.21,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the weakest route as written. rxn_008 cannot conserve the carbon skeleton in the stated one-step form: CH2O + HCN + NH3 contains only two carbons, while serine nitrile has three, so an additional carbon source or a prior C-C building step is required. The hydrothermal literature supports amino-acid synthesis but also warns that serine is unstable at high temperature, so this branch should stop at a precursor or include rapid export and cooling before hydrolysis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add the missing carbon source or start the hydrothermal branch from glycolaldehyde or glycolonitrile instead of one formaldehyde.\",\n        \"Consider terminating the hydrothermal branch at serine nitrile or another precursor, then export and cool before hydrolysis.\",\n        \"Justify hydrothermal feedstock concentrations and include the known serine-decomposition risk.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"